findings.detail:
cssBlock (lines 22-27) finds the block via `css.indexOf(`${selector} {`)` and ends it at the first `
}`. This is a load-bearing gate (the CSS-mirror sync + WCAG contrast contract), and its failure mode is SILENT over-capture, not a loud failure: if the token blocks are ever moved inside `@layer base { :root { … } }` or an `@media` wrapper — a routine Tailwind v4 refactor — the block's own closer becomes `
  }` (indented), so indexOf('
}') skips past it and the returned 'light' slice swallows every following rule up to the wrapper's close, including the `.dark` block. parseOklchToken's match() then returns whichever definition appears FIRST in that oversized slice, so depending on source order the light-theme contrast assertions can validate dark values (or vice versa) while everything stays green. Same class of problem if a second `:root {` block appears earlier in the file (indexOf takes the first) or a selector like `.dark .something {` precedes the real `.dark {`. Prettier's current flat 2-space formatting is the only thing making this correct today.
